WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.850
If you want to test a scientific experiment
in zero-g, you've got a few options.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.090
You could send it to the
International Space Station,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.639
but thats expensive, and, let's be honest,
it's unlikely to happen

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.209
unless you've got a few friends
at a space agency.

00:00:13.209 --> 00:00:16.490
You could package it up into a small Cubesat
and launch it,

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.800
but that's still pretty expensive.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.460
You could take it along on a zero-g flight,

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.990
and strap yourself into a plane that's accelerating
towards the ground

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.390
at just the right speed to cause weightlessness.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.921
But that's still a lot of hassle, and a lot
of expense, and a lot of fuel burned.

00:00:30.921 --> 00:00:35.190
But fortunately, there is another option.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.530
This is the Drop Tower at the
University of Bremen in Germany.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:41.610
Inside is an enormous vacuum chamber: and
for the last 90 minutes,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.460
powerful pumps have been have been removing
all the air from inside,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.010
turning it into a near-vacuum that's perfect
for freefall experiments.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.060
And there's a drop capsule
about ready to go.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:58.920
In this experiment, I try to study the charge
separation process through tribocharging,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.380
the effect that causes static electricity.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:08.360
During the microgravity, glass beads are released
into a capacitor.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.890
In microgravity, you have enough time to trace
your particles,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.180
and you can observe other interesting effects

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.470
that aren't observable in normal gravity.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.909
Acceleration due to gravity on Earth is roughly
10 metres per second per second,

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:29.869
and without air resistance to slow things
down,

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:32.619
that drop capsule is going to start moving
very quickly indeed.

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:37.880
In the first second after release, the drop
capsule goes 10 metres.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.380
Next second, 20.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.470
Next second, 30.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.700
Next second, 40,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.840
and then we've used up pretty much all the
height there is.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.580
To get just one more second's worth of freefall,
you'd have to build this tower half as high

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.410
again.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:56.100
Unless you do something really clever, and
start by going up.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.670
The experiments are installed into a drop
vehicle.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.939
That's the experiment itself,

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:03.310
it's all the measurement and diagnostic tools,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.979
it's the computer controlling it.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.289
We throw it upwards with up to almost 30g
for 200ms,

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.849
and then into zero-g for almost ten seconds,
then.

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:18.680
And the deceleration, finally, by the end
of the experiment

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.900
has a value of about 35 to maximum 50g.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.310
It should be very accurate, because you can
imagine,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.450
if the energy is too large, we would hit the
ceiling,

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.180
we would destroy our vacuum containment,

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:36.750
which is the most delicate element we have
in our house.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:42.001
We almost perfectly balance the centre of
gravity to the vertical axis of the capsule

00:02:42.001 --> 00:02:46.910
by adjusting some counterweights at appropriate
locations in the capsule.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.150
The experiments are in fluid mechanics, it's
in combustion,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.060
it's in fluid handling, it's in biotechnology,

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.990
it's in physics and astrophysics, it's in
fundamental physics.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.769
So almost any disciplines.

00:03:01.769 --> 00:03:05.440
It's a little counterintuitive, but the capsule
really is weightless:

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.430
all the way up and all the way down.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.910
The forces acting on it remain the same,

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.739
even as it changes direction relative to us.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:14.680
So here we go.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.680
Launch time.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.959
The slingshot will fire, and in the nine seconds
that the capsule's in freefall,

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:22.160
an eight-metre-deep pit filled with polystyrene
pellets

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.640
will slide in over the catapult to catch the
capsule.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.040
Hopefully!

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.520
Thanks to everyone at the University of Bremen,
at the Drop Tower,

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.709
and to the researchers who were testing here
today!

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:49.420
Pull down the description for links about
them and about their work.

